  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (2.4 x^2 + 0.6 z^2)/2, drawn at 0.4 times the loss. x (left-right) is the steep direction. Without noise, x is multiplied by 1 - 2.4 × rate each step, so swings grow once the rate is above 0.83.",
    "Each step = -rate × (true slope + noise). The noise is Gaussian and centred on zero, with SD 1.2 × sqrt((64-b)/(63 b)) per axis: 1.2 at batch 1 and exactly 0 at 64. A step lowers the loss only on average.",
    "Both arrows are steps drawn at 4 times their real length. Mini-batch step is the move the ball makes next. Full-data step is -rate × true slope. The length readout is the real, unscaled step.",
    "A run ends after 40 steps (finished), or as soon as the loss goes above 5 (diverged). The trail holds the start point plus one point per step. Points off the drawn bowl are shown at its rim.",
    "Noise comes from a fixed seed, so Reset replays the same values. New run, keep last path uses the next values and keeps the finished path as the faded ghost."
  ]
}